                <pb facs="0565" n="495" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg495"/>

                <head>No. 287.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. West
                        to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-03-10"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 10, 1883</date>.
                        (Received March 13.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of the 18th of
                    January last, I have the honor to inform you that Earl Granville has requested
                    me to communicate to you the substance of a dispatch received from Her Majesty’s
                    minister at Caracas, which is to the following effect: Mr. Baker, the United
                    States representative, has informed Her Majesty’s minister that he will submit
                    to him the proposals which he contemplates making to the Venezuelan Government
                    with regard to the custody of ships’ papers; and should they appear to meet the
                    circumstances of the case, Her Majesty’s minister, in compliance with his
                    instructions, will support Mr. Baker in a note to be addressed to the Venezuelan
                    Government.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>L. S. SACKVILLE WEST.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
